---
import BaseLayout from '../../../../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../../../../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import JcecPhase3Progress from '../../../../../../components/JcecPhase3Progress.astro';
import { phase3ThermalSites } from '../../../../../../data/jabberwockyPhase3';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<BaseLayout title="Mission 4: Control the Habitat | Jabberwocky 2190" description="Phase 3 Mission 4: use feedback, passive controls and thermal technologies to keep a habitat within a useful temperature range." themeColor="#081722">
  <div class="mission-shell" id="phase3-mission4">
    <Breadcrumbs items={[
      { label: 'Home', href: base },
      { label: 'Grade 7 Science', href: `${base}courses/grade-7-science/` },
      { label: 'Jabberwocky', href: `${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/` },
      { label: 'Phase 3', href: `${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/phase-3/` },
      { label: 'Mission 4' }
    ]} />

    <JcecPhase3Progress active={4} compact={true} />

    <header class="mission-hero">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">PHASE 3 // MISSION 4 OF 5</p>
        <h1>CONTROL THE <span>HABITAT</span></h1>
        <p class="question">How do humans keep temperatures within a useful range?</p>
      </div>
      <aside>
        <span>YOU ARE LEARNING</span>
        <strong>How feedback, passive controls and active systems respond to changing temperature</strong>
        <small>4 classes × 45 minutes</small>
      </aside>
    </header>

    <section class="mission-at-a-glance">
      <div><small>WHERE AM I?</small><strong>Mission 4 of 5</strong></div>
      <div><small>WHAT DO I HAND IN?</small><strong>1 Habitat Control Protocol + 1 reflection</strong></div>
      <div><small>WHY DOES IT MATTER?</small><strong>Insulation slows transfer, but a safe habitat still needs a system that notices change and responds.</strong></div>
    </section>

    <nav class="step-nav" aria-label="Mission 4 steps">
      <a href="#your-mission"><b>1</b><span>Your Mission</span></a>
      <a href="#learn"><b>2</b><span>Learn</span></a>
      <a href="#investigate"><b>3</b><span>Investigate</span></a>
      <a href="#decide"><b>4</b><span>Decide</span></a>
      <a href="#record"><b>5</b><span>Record It</span></a>
    </nav>

    <section class="simple-step" id="your-mission">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>1</span><div><p>CLASS 1</p><h2>Your Mission</h2></div></div>
      <div class="briefing">
        <p>Your team has already learned how to slow unwanted thermal-energy transfer. But insulation alone cannot keep a habitat safe under every condition. JCEC now needs a control system that notices when temperature leaves a useful range, chooses the least wasteful response and checks again. Build a simple control rule, test it with simulated temperature readings and decide what should respond first on your continent.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="assignment-result" id="assignment-result" hidden>
        <span id="assignment-icon"></span>
        <div><small>YOUR PHASE 3 CONTINENT</small><strong id="assignment-name"></strong><p id="assignment-subtitle"></p></div>
        <div class="challenge-badge"><small>THERMAL CHALLENGE</small><b id="assignment-challenge"></b></div>
      </div>
      <div class="carry-forward-note">
        <strong>Mission 3 still matters, but do not recreate it.</strong>
        <span>Your barrier recommendation is the passive starting point. Mission 4 asks what the habitat should do when temperature changes anyway.</span>
      </div>
      <div class="missing-assignment" id="missing-assignment" hidden>
        <strong>Your Phase 3 posting is not saved on this device yet.</strong>
        <p>Return to the Phase 3 Hub and choose the continent your teacher assigned. Do not create a new posting for this mission.</p>
        <a href={`${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/phase-3/`}>Get Phase 3 posting →</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="simple-step" id="learn">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>2</span><div><p>CLASSES 1 & 3</p><h2>Learn the Science</h2></div></div>
      <p class="step-intro"><strong>One connected system:</strong> a habitat can generate, transfer, remove and control thermal energy.</p>

      <div class="science-grid four">
        <article><span>🔥</span><strong>GENERATE</strong><p>Heaters and furnaces add thermal energy to a space. Combustion is one possible source; other systems can transfer energy from elsewhere.</p></article>
        <article><span>↔️</span><strong>TRANSFER</strong><p>Ducts, pipes, moving air and materials can move thermal energy from one place to another.</p></article>
        <article><span>❄️</span><strong>REMOVE</strong><p>Refrigerators and air conditioners move thermal energy out of a cooler space and release it somewhere else. They do not “make cold.”</p></article>
        <article><span>🎛️</span><strong>CONTROL</strong><p>A thermostat measures temperature, compares it with a target and tells a system when to respond.</p></article>
      </div>

      <div class="control-loop" aria-label="Thermostat feedback loop">
        <span>MEASURE</span><b>→</b><span>COMPARE WITH TARGET</span><b>→</b><span>RESPOND</span><b>→</b><span>MEASURE AGAIN</span>
      </div>

      <div class="device-grid">
        <article><small>THERMOMETER</small><strong>Measures</strong><p>Tells the system what the temperature is now.</p></article>
        <article><small>THERMOSTAT</small><strong>Decides when to respond</strong><p>Uses a target or safe range to switch actions on or off.</p></article>
        <article><small>HEATER / FURNACE</small><strong>Adds thermal energy</strong><p>Useful when the habitat is too cold.</p></article>
        <article><small>REFRIGERATOR / AIR CONDITIONER</small><strong>Moves thermal energy out</strong><p>Useful when a space must be kept cooler than its surroundings.</p></article>
      </div>

      <details class="help-box">
        <summary>Need Help? What makes this a feedback system?</summary>
        <div>
          <p>A thermostat does more than turn a machine on. It keeps checking what happened after the last response.</p>
          <p>If the temperature is still outside the target range, the system may keep responding. Once the target is reached, the active system can switch off.</p>
          <p>This repeated <strong>measure → compare → respond → measure again</strong> pattern is called feedback.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <section class="technology-strip">
        <div><p class="mini-label">THEN → NOW THERMAL TECHNOLOGY</p><h3>Human needs shaped thermal technology.</h3></div>
        <div class="then-now-grid">
          <article><small>NEED: STAY WARM</small><strong>Open fire / simple shelter</strong><b>→</b><span>controlled heaters, furnaces and thermostats</span></article>
          <article><small>NEED: KEEP THINGS COOL</small><strong>shade, cool storage, ice</strong><b>→</b><span>refrigerators and air-conditioning systems</span></article>
          <article><small>NEED: USE SUNLIGHT</small><strong>orientation and sun-facing surfaces</strong><b>→</b><span>passive solar design and active solar collectors</span></article>
        </div>
        <p class="technology-note">The point is not to memorize a timeline. Notice the pattern: <strong>human problem → thermal technology → new benefits and new trade-offs.</strong></p>
      </section>

      <section class="source-board">
        <div><p class="mini-label">THERMAL SOURCE BOARD</p><h3>Where can thermal energy come from?</h3></div>
        <div class="source-grid">
          <article><span>☀️</span><strong>Solar</strong><p>Radiant energy from a star can warm surfaces directly or be collected and transferred.</p></article>
          <article><span>🔥</span><strong>Combustion</strong><p>Burning fuel releases thermal energy, but also introduces safety and environmental trade-offs.</p></article>
          <article><span>🌋</span><strong>Geothermal</strong><p>Thermal energy stored within a planet can sometimes be used where suitable conditions exist.</p></article>
          <article><span>🌱</span><strong>Biological / living systems</strong><p>Living processes and decomposition can release thermal energy on a smaller scale.</p></article>
        </div>
        <p class="source-note">A source appearing here does <strong>not</strong> mean it is available on every Jabberwocky continent. Mission 5 will compare source choices more carefully.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="solar-compare">
        <article><small>PASSIVE SOLAR</small><strong>Use sunlight through design</strong><p>Examples: orientation, windows, shade and surfaces that change how much solar energy enters. No pump or collector is required to move the energy.</p></article>
        <article><small>ACTIVE SOLAR</small><strong>Collect and move solar energy</strong><p>A collector captures solar energy and a system moves the energy or heated fluid where it is needed.</p></article>
      </section>

      <div class="safety-strip"><strong>Safety is part of control:</strong><span>hot surfaces can burn, combustion can create fire and air-quality risks, overheating can be unsafe, and thermal systems should switch off when they are no longer needed.</span></div>
    </section>

    <section class="simple-step" id="investigate">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>3</span><div><p>CLASSES 1–3</p><h2>Investigate</h2></div></div>

      <section class="investigation-block">
        <div class="block-title"><div><small>MAIN INVESTIGATION</small><h3>Habitat Control Loop Challenge</h3></div><span>TEAM WORK</span></div>
        <p>This is a control-system simulation, not another insulation lab. Your team receives temperature readings, compares them with a safe range and chooses the response that should happen next.</p>

        <div class="target-range"><small>TRAINING TARGET RANGE</small><strong>18°C–22°C</strong><span>Your teacher may change the practice range. This is a simulation target, not a new continent climate fact.</span></div>

        <div class="action-cards">
          <article><strong>HEATER ON</strong><small>+2 energy units</small></article>
          <article><strong>HEATER OFF</strong><small>0 units</small></article>
          <article><strong>COOLING ON</strong><small>+3 energy units</small></article>
          <article><strong>COOLING OFF</strong><small>0 units</small></article>
          <article><strong>SHADE / UNSHADE</strong><small>passive</small></article>
          <article><strong>VENT / CLOSE VENT</strong><small>passive in this model</small></article>
        </div>

        <div class="loop-steps">
          <article><b>1</b><p>Read the current habitat temperature.</p></article>
          <article><b>2</b><p>Compare it with the target range.</p></article>
          <article><b>3</b><p>Choose one response card and explain why.</p></article>
          <article><b>4</b><p>Record any active-energy cost.</p></article>
          <article><b>5</b><p>Read the next training value and repeat.</p></article>
        </div>

        <div class="simulation-table">
          <div class="sim-head"><span>ROUND</span><span>READING</span><span>YOUR RESPONSE</span><span>WHY?</span></div>
          <div><strong>1</strong><span>17°C</span><span>____________</span><span>________________</span></div>
          <div><strong>2</strong><span>19°C</span><span>____________</span><span>________________</span></div>
          <div><strong>3</strong><span>23°C</span><span>____________</span><span>________________</span></div>
          <div><strong>4</strong><span>24°C</span><span>____________</span><span>________________</span></div>
          <div><strong>5</strong><span>21°C</span><span>____________</span><span>________________</span></div>
          <div><strong>6</strong><span>16°C</span><span>____________</span><span>________________</span></div>
        </div>
        <p class="model-note"><strong>Model limit:</strong> the next reading is supplied by the training simulation; it is not a precise prediction caused by your previous card. The science goal is to build a sensible feedback rule, not calculate a real HVAC system.</p>

        <section class="rule-builder">
          <p class="mini-label">BUILD YOUR CONTROL RULE</p>
          <div><strong>If the temperature falls below ______, then ______.</strong></div>
          <div><strong>If the temperature rises above ______, then ______.</strong></div>
          <div><strong>If the temperature is inside the target range, then ______.</strong></div>
        </section>

        <div class="fallback-note"><strong>Low-material rule:</strong><span>The core challenge needs only printed/projected readings, response cards and pencils. Thermometers, bimetal strips or other demonstrations are optional. No electronics or purchased thermostat equipment is required.</span></div>
        <button type="button" class="small-button" id="print-phase3-mission4">Print Mission 4 Control Loop + Protocol</button>
      </section>

      <section class="continent-case">
        <div class="block-title"><div><small>CLASS 3</small><h3>Your Continent Control Case</h3></div><span>3 CLUES ONLY</span></div>
        <p>Use the training loop to think about control. Then apply the idea to the real thermal challenge already established for your continent.</p>
        <div class="no-continent" id="no-continent"><strong>Phase 3 continent not found.</strong><a href={`${base}courses/grade-7-science/jabberwocky/phase-3/`}>Return to Phase 3 Hub →</a></div>
        <div id="control-files" hidden>
          {phase3ThermalSites.map((site) => (
            <article class="control-file" data-control-file={site.id} hidden>
              <header><span>{site.icon}</span><div><small>{site.name} · {site.subtitle}</small><h3>{site.thermalChallenge}</h3></div></header>
              <div class="clue-grid">{site.mission4Clues.map((clue, index) => <article><b>{index + 1}</b><p>{clue}</p></article>)}</div>
              <div class="restriction"><small>DEVELOPMENT LIMIT STILL ACTIVE</small><p>{site.previousTeamThermalBriefing.developmentRestriction}</p></div>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </section>
    </section>

    <section class="simple-step" id="decide">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>4</span><div><p>CLASSES 3–4</p><h2>Make a Decision</h2></div></div>
      <div class="decision-card">
        <p class="mini-label">ONE TEAM QUESTION</p>
        <h3>What should control the habitat temperature before JCEC spends more energy?</h3>
        <p class="decision-note">You may recommend a combination, but identify the first priority. Passive controls should be considered before active heating or cooling when they can solve the problem safely.</p>
        <div class="reasoning-frame three">
          <div><strong>CONDITION</strong><span>When the habitat is ______…</span></div>
          <div><strong>CONTROL RESPONSE</strong><span>…our system should ______…</span></div>
          <div><strong>EXPECTED RESULT</strong><span>…so the temperature should move/stay ______.</span></div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="simple-step" id="record">
      <div class="step-heading"><span>5</span><div><p>CLASS 4</p><h2>Record It</h2></div></div>
      <div class="handoff-strip"><span>👥 <strong>TEAM WORK</strong> control</span><i>→</i><span>📝 <strong>TEAM RECORD</strong> one protocol</span><i>→</i><span>💭 <strong>MY REFLECTION</strong> everyone answers</span></div>

      <div class="team-record">
        <div class="record-heading"><div><p class="mini-label">TEAM RECORD</p><h3>Habitat Control Protocol</h3></div><span>ONE PER TEAM</span></div>
        <p>Keep it short. This is a control rule another JCEC team could actually follow.</p>
        <div class="protocol-grid">
          <article><small>TARGET RANGE</small><strong>________ °C to ________ °C</strong></article>
          <article><small>PASSIVE CONTROL FIRST</small><strong>We use __________________ first.</strong></article>
          <article><small>WHEN HEATING ACTIVATES</small><strong>If __________________, heating __________________.</strong></article>
          <article><small>WHEN COOLING / VENTILATION ACTIVATES</small><strong>If __________________, __________________.</strong></article>
          <article><small>SAFETY RULE</small><strong>Humans must __________________.</strong></article>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="reflection-card">
        <p class="mini-label">MY REFLECTION</p>
        <h3>Why is a thermostat useful even when a habitat already has a heater or cooler?</h3>
        <p>Everyone answers individually. Explain the feedback idea, not just “because it turns it on.”</p>
      </div>

      <div class="finished-box">
        <strong>Mission 4 is finished when:</strong>
        <ul><li>your team has one Habitat Control Protocol;</li><li>your protocol uses a measured condition and a response rule;</li><li>you have completed your individual reflection.</li></ul>
        <p>Mission 5 — Survive Without Wasting It — is upcoming and locked.</p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

  <script is:inline define:vars={{ siteData: phase3ThermalSites }}>
    (() => {
      const PHASE3_POSTING_KEY = 'jabberwocky-phase3-posting';
      const sites = Object.fromEntries(siteData.map((site) => [site.id, site]));
      const saved = localStorage.getItem(PHASE3_POSTING_KEY);
      const assignmentResult = document.getElementById('assignment-result');
      const missing = document.getElementById('missing-assignment');
      const noContinent = document.getElementById('no-continent');
      const files = document.getElementById('control-files');
      if (saved && sites[saved]) {
        const site = sites[saved];
        assignmentResult.hidden = false;
        missing.hidden = true;
        document.getElementById('assignment-icon').textContent = site.icon;
        document.getElementById('assignment-name').textContent = site.name;
        document.getElementById('assignment-subtitle').textContent = site.subtitle;
        document.getElementById('assignment-challenge').textContent = site.thermalChallenge;
        noContinent.hidden = true;
        files.hidden = false;
        document.querySelectorAll('[data-control-file]').forEach((el) => { el.hidden = el.dataset.controlFile !== saved; });
      } else {
        assignmentResult.hidden = true;
        missing.hidden = false;
        noContinent.hidden = false;
        files.hidden = true;
      }
      const printBtn = document.getElementById('print-phase3-mission4');
      if (printBtn) printBtn.addEventListener('click', () => window.print());
    })();
  </script>
</BaseLayout>

